## Purpose / Description

I use this workflow to turn a local JSON file of farming or nonprofit-related job/company records into a cleaner lead list in Google Sheets. It reads the source file, separates each record, filters for entries that already look relevant and have a usable primary contact email, then processes each company one at a time so the workflow stays controlled and avoids overwhelming external services.

For each qualified record, I use an AI step to standardize or infer the company name from the available title and company description. I then check Google Sheets to see whether the primary email has already been filed, which helps prevent duplicate leads. If the lead is new, the workflow either saves the existing contact information directly or, when a website is available, sends the website to Apify’s contact details scraper to look for additional enriched contacts such as names, emails, phone numbers, and LinkedIn profiles.

The main tools involved are n8n for orchestration, local file reading and JSON extraction for the source data, filtering and batch processing nodes for quality control, an OpenAI-powered LLM chain with structured output parsing for company-name cleanup, Apify for website-based lead enrichment, and Google Sheets for storing and updating the final lead list. Wait-and-retry steps are included around spreadsheet actions to make the process more resilient if a temporary issue occurs.

This workflow can be adapted to many similar lead-building or research tasks. For example, I could use the same structure for schools, nonprofits, SaaS companies, agencies, clinics, or local businesses by changing the source data, the relevance filters, the AI prompt, the enrichment settings, and the destination spreadsheet columns. The overall pattern stays the same: load records, filter for quality, normalize key fields with AI, avoid duplicates, enrich from websites when possible, and save the best contact data into a central sheet.